WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.000
I married Joan, what a girl, what a world, what a life.

00:12.000 --> 00:19.000
Oh, I married Joan, what a mind, loving mind, what a wife.

00:19.000 --> 00:28.000
Doo doo doo, he is gay, all day she keeps my heart laughing, never know where her brain is going.

00:28.000 --> 00:35.000
Doo doo doo, he's in love, can't deny that's why I married Joan.

00:35.000 --> 00:39.000
The Joan Davis Show. I Married Joan.

00:39.000 --> 00:42.000
America's favorite comedy show.

00:42.000 --> 00:49.000
Starring America's queen of comedy, Joan Davis as Mrs. Joan Stevens.

00:49.000 --> 00:56.000
Doo doo doo, doo doo doo, doo doo doo.

00:56.000 --> 01:00.000
And featuring Jim Backus as Judge Bradley Stevens.

01:00.000 --> 01:06.000
Doo doo doo, doo doo doo, doo doo doo.

01:06.000 --> 01:12.000
I married Joan.

01:16.000 --> 01:18.000
Oh my gosh.

01:18.000 --> 01:22.000
Three thirty in the morning and they're still at it next door.

01:22.000 --> 01:25.000
Joanie, I'm going to call them up and complain.

01:27.000 --> 01:32.000
Honey, we've been complaining for six years now and you know it never does any good.

01:32.000 --> 01:35.000
Yeah, yeah, I guess you're right.

01:35.000 --> 01:40.000
Well, what do we do? Stay up all night as usual?

01:40.000 --> 01:42.000
Well, it's only three nights a week, dear.

01:42.000 --> 01:50.000
Saturday night's the big party, Tuesday night's the bridge game, Wednesday night the poker game.

01:50.000 --> 01:54.000
Only three nights a week.

01:54.000 --> 01:58.000
Well, what's the use? Can't go to sleep until the party's over next door and...

01:58.000 --> 02:02.000
Wait a minute, wait a minute, dear.

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
I don't hear anything now. Maybe it's over.

02:05.000 --> 02:07.000
Yes, yes, baby, you're right.

02:07.000 --> 02:08.000
Good night.

02:08.000 --> 02:10.000
Good night, love.

02:24.000 --> 02:27.000
Oh, all the dirty tricks.

02:27.000 --> 02:36.000
Joanie, I give up. We may as well have a party of our own and at least enjoy being stay-ups.

02:36.000 --> 02:39.000
A party? But I'm dead tired.

02:39.000 --> 02:45.000
You're tired? I'm so tired I can barely walk. I'll be right back, honey.

02:46.000 --> 02:50.000
I don't think this boy is well.

02:50.000 --> 02:58.000
Soft lights, my dear, and music and champagne.

03:02.000 --> 03:07.000
Good vintage. Piper Holstein, yesterday morning.

03:07.000 --> 03:10.000
Piper Holstein, my dear.

03:10.000 --> 03:17.000
I'm glad it wasn't flat.

03:17.000 --> 03:21.000
Mmm, good grapes.

03:21.000 --> 03:29.000
Champagne? I don't love it. The bubbles tickle my nose.

03:29.000 --> 03:36.000
And may I say, my dear, you've never looked more enchanting and ravishing.

03:36.000 --> 03:40.000
Thank you, my sweet. May I propose to...

03:40.000 --> 03:42.000
Do.

03:44.000 --> 03:50.000
Here's to our love. It could be inspired if we weren't so tired.

03:54.000 --> 04:00.000
Ah, my darling, would you... would you care to... to dance?

04:00.000 --> 04:03.000
No, thanks. I'd rather lie this one out.

04:03.000 --> 04:08.000
But darling, they're playing our song.

04:08.000 --> 04:10.000
Our song?

04:10.000 --> 04:38.000
Yes, my precious one, come and dance with me.

04:38.000 --> 04:43.000
Hello? Who? What?

04:47.000 --> 04:48.000
Well, how do you like that?

04:48.000 --> 04:49.000
Who was that?

04:49.000 --> 04:51.000
The car stares next door.

04:51.000 --> 04:56.000
They said they're trying to get to sleep now, and would we please stop making such a racket?

04:56.000 --> 04:59.000
Wait, wait, just a little more.

04:59.000 --> 05:01.000
Oh, I'm having my...

05:01.000 --> 05:04.000
Brad! Oh, Brad, wait, wait, let me hear the news.

05:04.000 --> 05:05.000
Well?

05:05.000 --> 05:10.000
Well, I went next door to the car stairs to complain, and guess what?

05:10.000 --> 05:11.000
What?

05:11.000 --> 05:12.000
They're moving!

05:12.000 --> 05:13.000
Moving?

05:13.000 --> 05:17.000
Yes! They feel it's too noisy in the neighborhood.

05:17.000 --> 05:20.000
Oh, they're moving, they're moving!

05:20.000 --> 05:21.000
They're moving?

05:21.000 --> 05:22.000
That's right!

05:22.000 --> 05:28.000
Yep, they put the house up for sale, and as soon as it's sold, we're going to be having new neighbors, darling.

05:28.000 --> 05:29.000
Isn't that wonderful?

05:29.000 --> 05:36.000
They say he'll be staying away every Saturday, Wednesday, and Thursday, and Friday, and all of them is moving.

05:38.000 --> 05:43.000
Goodbye forever, goodbye forever, goodbye forever.

05:43.000 --> 05:47.000
Joan, Joan, Joan.

05:47.000 --> 05:49.000
Hello?

05:49.000 --> 05:53.000
Well, how do you know what the new neighbors will be like?

05:53.000 --> 05:57.000
Oh, that's right. Gee, they might even be worse, huh?

05:57.000 --> 06:02.000
And after six years with the car stairs, we deserve something better.

06:02.000 --> 06:03.000
Brad?

06:03.000 --> 06:04.000
Yeah?

06:04.000 --> 06:06.000
There's only one thing to do.

06:06.000 --> 06:07.000
What?

06:07.000 --> 06:10.000
I'm going into the real estate business.

06:10.000 --> 06:13.000
What? The real estate business?

06:13.000 --> 06:15.000
Joey, what are you talking about?

06:15.000 --> 06:19.000
Well, I'm going to find a buyer for the car stairs house.

06:19.000 --> 06:22.000
That way we'll know what kind of neighbors we're getting.

06:24.000 --> 06:26.000
Three nights a week.

06:26.000 --> 06:31.000
And that's the situation. You can see how serious it is.

06:31.000 --> 06:34.000
You've just got to help me find a buyer for the car stairs house.

06:34.000 --> 06:36.000
We'll be glad to help you, Joan.

06:36.000 --> 06:37.000
Sure we will.

06:37.000 --> 06:38.000
Oh, I'd love to.

06:38.000 --> 06:39.000
I think we can.

06:39.000 --> 06:41.000
But just what kind of neighbors do you want?

06:41.000 --> 06:44.000
I don't care, as long as they're quiet.

06:44.000 --> 06:48.000
I was thinking possibly of a GI, a veteran.

06:48.000 --> 06:49.000
A veteran?

06:49.000 --> 06:52.000
Yeah, of the Civil War.

06:52.000 --> 06:55.000
That would make them pretty quiet.

06:55.000 --> 06:58.000
I hear Miriam Walters is looking for a house.

06:58.000 --> 07:00.000
Miriam Walters? I don't believe I know her.

07:00.000 --> 07:02.000
A ghastly woman.

07:02.000 --> 07:03.000
Is she quiet?

07:03.000 --> 07:05.000
And her husband is such a bore.

07:05.000 --> 07:06.000
Is he quiet?

07:06.000 --> 07:07.000
They're a horrible couple.

07:07.000 --> 07:08.000
Are they quiet?

07:08.000 --> 07:10.000
Now, let's forget about the Walters.

07:10.000 --> 07:12.000
He would never work.

07:12.000 --> 07:14.000
In the first place, she's an awful woman.

07:14.000 --> 07:16.000
Oh, girls, girls, just a minute, please.

07:16.000 --> 07:17.000
I know the very people.

07:17.000 --> 07:20.000
You girls all know Agatha Murdoch, don't you?

07:20.000 --> 07:21.000
Is she quiet?

07:21.000 --> 07:23.000
And her husband, Drexel Murdoch?

07:23.000 --> 07:24.000
Is he quiet?

07:24.000 --> 07:25.000
They're a wonderful couple.

07:25.000 --> 07:26.000
Are they quiet?

07:26.000 --> 07:27.000
They have a chauffeur.

07:27.000 --> 07:28.000
Is he quiet?

07:28.000 --> 07:29.000
And a big black limousine.

07:29.000 --> 07:30.000
Is it quiet?

07:30.000 --> 07:32.000
Oh, they're just what you want.

07:32.000 --> 07:34.000
They're a quiet, elderly couple.

07:34.000 --> 07:37.000
And I happen to know that Agatha is looking for a smaller house

07:37.000 --> 07:39.000
now that her children are all married.

07:39.000 --> 07:41.000
Now, girls, let's get on with this.

07:41.000 --> 07:43.000
I just can't thank you enough, Claire.

07:43.000 --> 07:45.000
That's fine.

07:45.000 --> 07:47.000
Now, there's many things to take up today.

07:47.000 --> 07:49.000
You don't even know what this means to me.

07:49.000 --> 07:50.000
Yes, we've just...

07:50.000 --> 07:51.000
Why?

07:51.000 --> 07:54.000
Now, Brad's been going out for 6 years all alone.

07:54.000 --> 07:56.000
It's just a little quiet.

07:56.000 --> 07:57.000
Quiet, see?

07:57.000 --> 07:58.000
Yes.

07:58.000 --> 07:59.000
You, see?

07:59.000 --> 08:01.000
Yes.

08:01.000 --> 08:05.000
Shhh.

08:05.000 --> 08:07.000
Brian, isn't it?

08:07.000 --> 08:09.000
Ah, it's a pleasure.

08:09.000 --> 08:13.000
Well, Ben, admit that you're married to a real little genius.

08:13.000 --> 08:14.000
Well...

08:14.000 --> 08:16.000
Go on, admit it, Eddie.

08:16.000 --> 08:19.000
Oh, all right, I'm married to a real little genius.

08:19.000 --> 08:20.000
You're darn right.

08:20.000 --> 08:24.000
Dear, the Carsons put their house up for sale hardly more than a week ago.

08:24.000 --> 08:28.000
And our new neighbors, the Murdochs, have already bought the house and moved in.

08:28.000 --> 08:30.000
Look, Joni, I'm tired. We've got to...

08:30.000 --> 08:33.000
And are they just ordinary neighbors?

08:33.000 --> 08:36.000
No, they're hand-picked neighbors.

08:36.000 --> 08:37.000
Yes, sirree.

08:37.000 --> 08:43.000
Hand-picked by that neighbor-picking expert, Joni Stevens.

08:43.000 --> 08:45.000
Yes, folks.

08:45.000 --> 08:48.000
I've been hand-picking neighbors now for now on to 20 years.

08:48.000 --> 08:54.000
And I can truthfully say that the Murdochs are just naturally milder.

08:54.000 --> 08:56.000
Quieter neighbors.

08:56.000 --> 09:01.000
Folks, if you'll notice, their lights are already out.

09:01.000 --> 09:10.000
Proving that the Murdochs are at least 30% older, quieter, and quieter neighbors.

09:10.000 --> 09:13.000
Try a Murdoch today, won't you?

09:13.000 --> 09:16.000
And now, back to our feature film.

09:16.000 --> 09:18.000
All right, Joni.

09:18.000 --> 09:22.000
You did a wonderful job, but let's take advantage of it and get some sleep.

09:22.000 --> 09:26.000
Okay, darling, but remember, when you're snoring your head off tonight,

09:26.000 --> 09:29.000
don't forget that you owe it all to me.

09:29.000 --> 09:31.000
Oh, yeah.

09:31.000 --> 09:33.000
Good night, dear.

09:33.000 --> 09:35.000
Ah.

09:35.000 --> 09:37.000
Oh.

09:37.000 --> 09:40.000
Mm-hmm.

09:40.000 --> 09:47.000
Dog.

09:47.000 --> 09:50.000
But who keeps dogs so close by?

09:50.000 --> 09:52.000
It's...

09:52.000 --> 09:54.000
Oh, my gosh.

09:54.000 --> 09:56.000
It's the Murdochs.

09:56.000 --> 09:59.000
They've got barking dogs.

09:59.000 --> 10:04.000
And dogs just don't make noise on Tuesday and Wednesday and Saturday nights.

10:04.000 --> 10:08.000
They can keep it up every night.

10:08.000 --> 10:11.000
I owe it all to you.

10:11.000 --> 10:16.000
Shh.

10:16.000 --> 10:25.000
Ah!

10:25.000 --> 10:26.000
Yes?

10:26.000 --> 10:28.000
What is it?

10:28.000 --> 10:33.000
Do you remember me, Clara's friend, Joan Stevens, your next-door neighbor?

10:33.000 --> 10:35.000
Well.

10:35.000 --> 10:38.000
I picked a fine time to pay a social call.

10:38.000 --> 10:40.000
Oh, no, no, this isn't a social call.

10:40.000 --> 10:42.000
It's about your dogs.

10:42.000 --> 10:44.000
My dogs?

10:44.000 --> 10:46.000
What about my dogs?

10:46.000 --> 10:49.000
Well, they're barking.

10:49.000 --> 10:53.000
My dear Mrs. Stevens, that is the normal course of things.

10:53.000 --> 10:59.000
Canaries sing, cats meow, dogs bark, and good night.

10:59.000 --> 11:01.000
Oh, just a minute.

11:01.000 --> 11:04.000
You don't understand.

11:04.000 --> 11:05.000
What?

11:05.000 --> 11:08.000
Well, my husband and I are trying to sleep.

11:08.000 --> 11:12.000
So could you please keep your dogs quiet?

11:12.000 --> 11:13.000
Keep them quiet?

11:13.000 --> 11:15.000
Yes.

11:15.000 --> 11:19.000
Are you suggesting that I repress my dogs?

11:19.000 --> 11:20.000
Inhibit them?

11:20.000 --> 11:23.000
Warp their personalities?

11:23.000 --> 11:26.000
My husband and I are trying to sleep.

11:26.000 --> 11:29.000
Mrs. Stevens, I'm afraid you don't understand.

11:29.000 --> 11:37.000
These are show dogs with the finest breeding and bloodlines that go back hundreds and hundreds of years.

11:37.000 --> 11:44.000
Do you expect me to deny sensitive animals the right to self-expression?

11:44.000 --> 11:47.000
Well, of course you don't.

11:47.000 --> 11:50.000
No, I guess not.

11:50.000 --> 11:54.000
So you just run along, Mrs. Stevens, and I'll tell you what.

11:54.000 --> 11:55.000
What?

11:55.000 --> 11:58.000
I won't even tell them that you complained.

11:58.000 --> 12:00.000
Oh, thank you.

12:00.000 --> 12:05.000
Good night, Mrs. Miller.

12:05.000 --> 12:07.000
Just a minute, Mrs. Mursh.

12:07.000 --> 12:09.000
Not so loud.

12:09.000 --> 12:12.000
These dogs are nervous and temperamental, remember?

12:12.000 --> 12:15.000
You'll upset them.

12:15.000 --> 12:16.000
What about my husband?

12:16.000 --> 12:19.000
He's trying to sleep.

12:19.000 --> 12:23.000
Mrs. Stevens, you have a most peculiar sense of values.

12:23.000 --> 12:29.000
Has your husband won six blue ribbons or a gold loving cup?

12:29.000 --> 12:32.000
No.

12:32.000 --> 12:39.000
Is your husband by Hannibal II out of Princess Moonbeam III?

12:39.000 --> 12:40.000
I don't think so.

12:40.000 --> 12:42.000
At least he never mentioned it.

12:42.000 --> 12:49.000
You see, Mrs. Stevens, we must prevent your husband from annoying my dogs.

12:49.000 --> 12:53.000
Well, of course. Now, don't you worry, Mrs. Maydock.

12:53.000 --> 12:57.000
I'll see to it that my husband doesn't bother them at all.

12:57.000 --> 12:58.000
Thank you very much.

12:58.000 --> 12:59.000
You're welcome.

12:59.000 --> 13:00.000
Good night.

13:00.000 --> 13:04.000
Good night, Mrs. Maydock.

13:04.000 --> 13:07.000
What happened here?

13:07.000 --> 13:14.000
So sleepy and groggy in court today, I signed the wrong paper and sentenced myself to jail for six months.

13:14.000 --> 13:16.000
Well, I'm pretty sleepy myself.

13:16.000 --> 13:22.000
All night long, those dogs barking, barking, barking.

13:22.000 --> 13:24.000
Just like that, they kept barking.

13:24.000 --> 13:26.000
Better at it again.

13:26.000 --> 13:28.000
Joan, Joan, you know what I said.

13:28.000 --> 13:30.000
I'll call the police.

13:30.000 --> 13:32.000
Can't. Hate to do this.

13:32.000 --> 13:36.000
And a man has to sleep.

13:36.000 --> 13:39.000
Joan, Joan, maybe you'd better speak to the police.

13:39.000 --> 13:44.000
As a judge, I don't want it to appear as though I'm taking advantage of my office.

13:44.000 --> 13:48.000
All right, dear.

13:48.000 --> 13:51.000
But remember, we're not going through another night like the last one.

13:51.000 --> 13:52.000
Now, be very firm.

13:52.000 --> 13:55.000
Oh, I will. I'll be a regular old...

13:55.000 --> 13:56.000
Hello.

13:56.000 --> 13:59.000
Fourth Street Station, Kilpatrick speaking.

13:59.000 --> 14:03.000
Hello, this is Mrs. Stevens at 133 Stone Drive.

14:03.000 --> 14:09.000
I'd like to complain about some dogs residing at 135 Stone Drive.

14:09.000 --> 14:12.000
They bark all night long and keep us awake.

14:12.000 --> 14:17.000
Well, madam, we don't like to send men out unless the dogs are actually disturbing the peace.

14:17.000 --> 14:19.000
Are they barking right now?

14:19.000 --> 14:23.000
Of course they're barking right now.

14:23.000 --> 14:28.000
Listen to this, officer.

14:28.000 --> 14:30.000
Did you hear that?

14:30.000 --> 14:32.000
No, I can't say I did.

14:32.000 --> 14:46.000
Come on.

14:46.000 --> 14:48.000
How about now?

14:48.000 --> 14:52.000
No.

14:52.000 --> 14:54.000
You can't hear them, Brad.

14:54.000 --> 14:55.000
Listen, I know what to do.

14:55.000 --> 14:56.000
This phone's got a long cord.

14:56.000 --> 14:58.000
I'll take it out in the backyard.

14:58.000 --> 15:00.000
Then that officer can really hear them.

15:00.000 --> 15:02.000
That's a good idea.

15:02.000 --> 15:05.000
One moment, please.

15:05.000 --> 15:08.000
Take it easy. Take it easy.

15:08.000 --> 15:13.000
One moment now, officer, and you'll hear the loudest barking you ever heard.

15:13.000 --> 15:16.000
Come here. Come here, fellas. Come here.

15:16.000 --> 15:21.000
Come on. Come on.

15:21.000 --> 15:25.000
Okay, boys, sound your ray.

15:25.000 --> 15:31.000
One at a time, please.

15:31.000 --> 15:33.000
We'll stick the phone in their faces.

15:33.000 --> 15:34.000
Maybe that'll make them bark.

15:34.000 --> 15:38.000
Yes, yes.

15:38.000 --> 15:41.000
He thinks it's a licorice bone.

15:41.000 --> 15:43.000
Come here, boys.

15:43.000 --> 15:45.000
Hello? Hello? Hello?

15:45.000 --> 15:47.000
Hello?

15:47.000 --> 15:51.000
They'll be barking in a moment now, officer.

15:51.000 --> 15:55.000
Come on, please. You can even growl. That's all right.

15:55.000 --> 15:58.000
Go ahead. Do something. Will you, fellas? Do a little something.

15:58.000 --> 16:03.000
Say hello. Anything at all. Anything.

16:03.000 --> 16:10.000
Look, I'll bark at you, then you bark at me, right? Okay?

16:10.000 --> 16:12.000
Now bark at me. Come on, bark. Bark.

16:12.000 --> 16:14.000
Come on, I'm barking.

16:14.000 --> 16:17.000
Oh, so that's your game, is it?

16:17.000 --> 16:21.000
You're playing yourself and trying to put the blame on some poor, innocent dogs.

16:21.000 --> 16:23.000
Oh, no, no, no, officer.

16:23.000 --> 16:25.000
No more of your lies now.

16:25.000 --> 16:31.000
Go on to bed, lady, and sober up.

16:31.000 --> 16:37.000
I got to go back to bed and sober up, Brad.

16:37.000 --> 16:39.000
Isn't that the limit, Brad?

16:39.000 --> 16:41.000
Just when you want the dogs to bark, they won't bark.

16:41.000 --> 16:43.000
Well, that's animals, honey.

16:43.000 --> 16:47.000
Well, at least now that it's quiet, maybe we can get some sleep tonight, huh?

16:47.000 --> 16:54.000
Oh, boy, I sure need it.

16:54.000 --> 17:09.000
Oh.

17:09.000 --> 17:15.000
Honey, I'm home.

17:15.000 --> 17:17.000
Oh, hello, dear.

17:17.000 --> 17:21.000
Hello, darling.

17:21.000 --> 17:22.000
Dinner's ready.

17:22.000 --> 17:24.000
Oh, I'm too tired, dear.

17:24.000 --> 17:27.000
So exhausted, I can't see straight.

17:27.000 --> 17:32.000
And tomorrow I have to render a verdict on the most important case that's ever come up before me.

17:32.000 --> 17:35.000
Oh, I just feel awful, Brad. I chose those neighbors.

17:35.000 --> 17:39.000
Well, darling, I just simply have to get some sleep.

17:39.000 --> 17:41.000
Well, you go right to bed, darling.

17:41.000 --> 17:44.000
I'll figure out some way to quiet those dogs.

17:44.000 --> 17:46.000
Oh, dear.

17:46.000 --> 17:48.000
No, no, dear, no.

17:48.000 --> 17:51.000
No, honey, the bedroom's over this way, dear.

17:51.000 --> 18:10.000
Oh, dear.

18:10.000 --> 18:13.000
Hello, Dr. Schaefer speaking.

18:13.000 --> 18:16.000
Is this Dr. Schaefer, the veterinarian?

18:16.000 --> 18:17.000
Yes.

18:17.000 --> 18:20.000
Doctor, I wonder if you could answer a question for me.

18:20.000 --> 18:23.000
Now, what makes dogs bark at night?

18:23.000 --> 18:25.000
That's really quite simple, madam.

18:25.000 --> 18:27.000
Dogs are very much like babies.

18:27.000 --> 18:31.000
They bark at night for the same reason that a baby cries.

18:31.000 --> 18:34.000
You mean you have to burp them or change them?

18:34.000 --> 18:37.000
No, no, I mean they want attention.

18:37.000 --> 18:38.000
They're lonesome.

18:38.000 --> 18:40.000
They want company, that's all.

18:40.000 --> 18:41.000
Oh, yes, that's right.

18:41.000 --> 18:45.000
I remember when I was out with them last night, they stopped barking.

18:45.000 --> 18:48.000
In fact, I couldn't even get them to bark.

18:48.000 --> 18:51.000
That proves my point exactly.

18:51.000 --> 18:52.000
Well, thanks.

18:52.000 --> 18:55.000
I guess I know what I have to do.

18:55.000 --> 19:07.000
Thank you.

19:07.000 --> 19:09.000
Why don't you get undressed, dear?

19:09.000 --> 19:11.000
You'll be more comfortable.

19:11.000 --> 19:14.000
Joni, aren't you going to sleep?

19:14.000 --> 19:17.000
I'm going over to Mabel's for a little while, dear.

19:17.000 --> 19:19.000
Wait up for me, dear.

19:47.000 --> 19:55.000
Well, how are you, fellas?

19:55.000 --> 19:57.000
Which way to the dormitory?

19:57.000 --> 19:59.000
Oh, here it is.

19:59.000 --> 20:03.000
Well, nice little place you got here, huh, boys?

20:03.000 --> 20:05.000
What do you call it, early Caleonial?

20:05.000 --> 20:07.000
Now, listen, fellas.

20:07.000 --> 20:08.000
Listen, come here.

20:08.000 --> 20:09.000
Come out here.

20:09.000 --> 20:10.000
Come out here.

20:10.000 --> 20:11.000
I want to talk to you a minute.

20:11.000 --> 20:15.000
I came over here to spend the evening with you two so you wouldn't bark, see?

20:15.000 --> 20:17.000
Now, I don't want one peep out of you.

20:17.000 --> 20:18.000
No more.

20:18.000 --> 20:20.000
Will you shake on that?

20:20.000 --> 20:22.000
Let's shake.

20:22.000 --> 20:24.000
Now, you promised me there'd be no barking.

20:24.000 --> 20:27.000
Now, shall we all go to bed like good doggies?

20:27.000 --> 20:29.000
All right.

20:29.000 --> 20:30.000
Excuse me.

20:30.000 --> 20:31.000
Excuse me.

20:31.000 --> 20:34.000
Let's all get in here now.

20:34.000 --> 20:35.000
Excuse me, please.

20:35.000 --> 20:37.000
All right.

20:37.000 --> 20:38.000
Let's all get in.

20:38.000 --> 20:40.000
I tell you, you come out here.

20:40.000 --> 20:41.000
Here, come on out.

20:41.000 --> 20:49.000
I'll get in first and then you can get in.

20:49.000 --> 20:52.000
My, it sure is dusty in here.

20:52.000 --> 20:55.000
But I guess you weren't expecting company, huh?

20:55.000 --> 20:58.000
Well, let's all go to sleep now.

20:58.000 --> 21:00.000
Turn out the lights.

21:00.000 --> 21:04.000
Oh, I guess you got no electricity in here, huh?

21:04.000 --> 21:09.000
Let's all be good boys now and get a little shut eye.

21:09.000 --> 21:12.000
Ow!

21:12.000 --> 21:16.000
Why don't you bury your bones like the rest of us?

21:16.000 --> 21:18.000
Oh, well, good night, dear.

21:18.000 --> 21:24.000
Oh, thank you, dear.

21:24.000 --> 21:26.000
Good night, sweetheart.

21:26.000 --> 21:28.000
Come on, baby.

21:28.000 --> 21:29.000
There you are.

21:29.000 --> 21:31.000
Eat your bite of this.

21:31.000 --> 21:33.000
Come on, Montgomery.

21:33.000 --> 21:35.000
I've got a nice tidbit for you, too.

21:35.000 --> 21:39.000
Go get the bone, Montgomery. It's delicious.

21:39.000 --> 21:41.000
Now don't make me angry.

21:41.000 --> 21:43.000
Go get the stone, it's ours.

21:43.000 --> 21:45.000
Go get the bone, Montgomery.

21:45.000 --> 21:54.000
Montgomery, don't make me come in after you.

21:54.000 --> 21:58.000
There, there, there, there.

21:58.000 --> 22:00.000
I really wasn't going to hurt you.

22:00.000 --> 22:08.000
You know how much I love you, Montgomery.

22:08.000 --> 22:10.000
Montgomery, give me a little kiss.

22:10.000 --> 22:12.000
Come on.

22:12.000 --> 22:14.000
There you go, baby.

22:14.000 --> 22:16.000
Good night, Montgomery.

22:16.000 --> 22:20.000
Good night, Mrs. Murdoch.

22:20.000 --> 22:24.000
Montgomery, what did you say?

22:24.000 --> 22:30.000
I said, I'm sorry.

22:30.000 --> 22:31.000
That's better.

22:31.000 --> 22:37.000
Come on.

22:37.000 --> 22:41.000
Oh, you little...

22:41.000 --> 22:43.000
Good night.

22:43.000 --> 23:11.000
Oh, fine.

23:11.000 --> 23:14.000
Oh, good morning, Joan.

23:14.000 --> 23:16.000
Oh, good morning, dear.

23:16.000 --> 23:18.000
You had a good night's sleep, didn't you?

23:18.000 --> 23:23.000
Yeah, yeah, I slept fine, except those dogs didn't bark once, darn it.

23:23.000 --> 23:25.000
What do you mean, darn it?

23:25.000 --> 23:28.000
Well, you remember when you went over to Mabel's last night?

23:28.000 --> 23:29.000
Oh, yes, Mabel.

23:29.000 --> 23:35.000
Yeah, well, I phoned the police and swore on my word as a judge that those dogs would be barking last night.

23:35.000 --> 23:41.000
So I sent a man out here who spent the whole night in the backyard waiting for those dogs to bark.

23:41.000 --> 23:44.000
Now they'll never send a man out here again.

23:44.000 --> 23:47.000
I wonder why those dogs didn't bark last night.

23:47.000 --> 23:50.000
Well, maybe they had company, dear.

23:50.000 --> 23:52.000
Maybe they had a babysitter.

23:52.000 --> 23:54.000
Are you kidding?

23:54.000 --> 23:57.000
Who could be that stupid?

23:57.000 --> 23:59.000
You never know, dear.

23:59.000 --> 24:09.000
You never know.

24:09.000 --> 24:29.000
Oh, my Mary Joan, what a girl, what a world, what a life. Oh, my Mary Joan, what a mind, love, and spine, what a wife to.

24:29.000 --> 24:42.000
To each his own, and he neither for our Mary Joan. To each his own, and he neither for our Mary Joan.

